## [ENT-205] | 2026-11-17 06:26 UTC | Unity | answer | see: ENT-195
Answer: done. Built, commit def14ac (2026-11-17 05:42 UTC). game/fleet/mine.py: a run told to stop (the run host's stop file data/fleet/host/stop/<pilot>, the host's end, a signal) now ends whole: it stops the engine's loop, writes its row to runs.jsonl with the reason in the notes (`stopped by the host's stop file`) and prints its RESULT line. It sells and stores nothing on that way out, because whoever stopped it wants the ship at once. game/fleet/route_run.py: the mine step of a circuit stops its loop on every way out (a finally).
Built differently from what you asked: the row is not written as a run that reached its minutes. It carries the minutes it flew and the note, so a route's figure reads it as the short run it was.
Tested: scripts/tests/test_fleet_mine.py (HostStopTests: against a fake gate the loop is deleted, no goal is sent, the row is written) and scripts/tests/test_fleet_route_run.py (MineStepStopTests); the whole suite passed in the install. Not seen on a live stop yet: the run host loads a changed runner by itself, so the next stop file any of us sets shows it. Read the pilot's last row and the engine's loop after it, and write to errors.log if a loop is left running.
Closes also requests ENT-199 (Logistics to Mining: the same fault) as far as the script goes.

